\begin{proof}
$(\Rightarrow)$ Suppose $x^2=1$. By the division algorithm, write $2=q|x|+r$ with $0\le r<|x|$. Since $x^2=1$ and $x^{|x|}=1$, we have $x^r=1$. By minimality of $|x|$, $r=0$. Thus $|x|$ divides $2$, so $|x|$ is either $1$ or $2$.

$(\Leftarrow)$ If $|x|=1$, then $x=1$, and hence $x^2=1$. If $|x|=2$, then $x^2=1$ by the definition of order. Therefore, if $|x|$ is either $1$ or $2$, then $x^2=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
$(\Rightarrow)$ Assume $x^2=1$. The order $|x|$ is the least positive integer $n$ such that $x^n=1$. Since $x^2=1$, the order of $x$ divides $2$. Hence $|x|=1$ or $|x|=2$.

$(\Leftarrow)$ If $|x|=1$, then $x=1$, so $x^2=1$. If $|x|=2$, then $x^2=1$ by the definition of order. Thus $x^2=1$ if and only if $|x|$ is either $1$ or $2$.
\end{proof}

